</tbody></table></div>
<p>First, the vacancy. 451,792 of 1,054,000 sq ft, about 43% of the building, is being actively marketed, which is what "partly occupied" means in this report and is the closest thing to a measured baseline that exists.<a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a></p>
<p>Second, the listing quantifies the grid constraint from the property's own side. The building is served at 4,000 A, 480 V, 3-phase, roughly 3.3 MVA, and its broker advertises 6 to 8 MW of additional power as available.<a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a> Volition's permitted 393.25 MW is ~50 to 65 times that headroom, and the ordinance's 500 MW cap is 60 to 80 times it.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></p>
<p>And the 36 dock doors are the truck-traffic argument. A building with 36 dock-high positions and one grade door was designed for continuous trailer cycling. The data center uses none of them.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></p>
  <h3 id="the-alternative-use-side-by-side">The alternative use, side by side</h3>
<p><strong>The counterfactual is this building operating as designed, not an empty field.</strong></p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 08</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
